module stdlib.time

# The two clocks. Declared here rather than in the prelude: nothing in the prelude calls them, and
# under the rule "an extern lives in the module that owns its surface" this is that module.
#
# `export` on an `extern fn` would be inert: the parser discards it (parser.sprout:1941) and the
# bundler never registers extern names (bundler.sprout:597), so an extern is reachable by bare name
# from anywhere this module is bundled. The wrapper API below is what makes that reach intentional
# and greppable, not a visibility rule.
#
# This module once carried a "NO TYPE DECLARATIONS, deliberately" rule, cross-referencing the same
# note in stdlib/fs.sprout: a `type` would have dropped the module out of the REPL, whose loader
# validated type-decl field names against a fixed vocabulary and swallowed the failure. That
# constraint is GONE — Fix A of docs/repl-env-type-vocabulary-v0.md (`@type:` env markers) retired
# it, and stdlib.fs now declares types and still loads. Declare a type here if one is warranted.
extern fn time_now_micros() -> Int !{IO}
extern fn wall_time_micros() -> Int !{IO}

# CLOCK_MONOTONIC. The epoch is UNSPECIFIED — only the difference between two calls carries
# meaning. This is the clock for elapsed time: timeouts, deadlines, benchmarks.
export fn now_micros() -> Int !{IO} =
  time_now_micros()

# CLOCK_REALTIME: microseconds since the Unix epoch. This is the clock for civil time — log
# timestamps, `format_iso8601`, anything a human reads as a date.
#
# It is NOT monotonic. NTP slew and manual clock changes can move it backwards, so a duration
# computed from two of these values can come out negative or wildly wrong. Never subtract wall
# times to measure elapsed time; that is what `now_micros` is for. The two names are one keystroke
# apart at a call site, and picking the wrong one produces a bug that stays invisible until the
# clock is adjusted — which is why they are documented as a pair rather than separately.
export fn wall_micros() -> Int !{IO} =
  wall_time_micros()
